'\" t
.\" Probe: view with man ./rule-join.1 to see how this system's roff draws
.\" a header rule.  Table A is roffsub form.  Table B is outside roffsub:
.\" its rule row is a row of cell rules under a format line with a vertical
.\" bar, which roff may draw as a + where the bar crosses the rule.
.TH RULE-JOIN 7 2026-11-15 probe probe
.SH TABLE A
.TS
tab(@);
lb lb
l  l.
OPTION@MEANING
_
\fB\-r\fR@reverse the order
\fB\-k\fR \fIKEY\fR@sort on \fIKEY\fR
.TE
.SH TABLE B
.TS
tab(@);
lb lb
l|l
l  l.
OPTION@MEANING
_@_
\fB\-r\fR@reverse the order
\fB\-k\fR \fIKEY\fR@sort on \fIKEY\fR
.TE
.SH TABLE C
.\" Table C stays inside tbl's own features: a three-cell separator column
.\" with zero column separation on both sides.  Data rows leave it empty
.\" (a three-space gap); the rule row writes \-+\- there, joining the two
.\" cell rules.  No vertical bar is drawn anywhere.
.TS
tab(@);
lb0 cb0 lb
l0  c0  l.
OPTION@@MEANING
_@\-+\-@_
\fB\-r\fR@@reverse the order
\fB\-k\fR \fIKEY\fR@@sort on \fIKEY\fR
.TE
